,title,link,text,language,family
0,Akabo dialect - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akabo_dialect,"Akabo or Bo is an extinct dialect of the Northern Andamanese language. It was spoken on the west central coast of North Andaman and on North Reef Island of the Andaman Islands in India. It was recorded as being mutually intelligible with Aka-Jeru, and the vocabularies are very similar.",Aka-Bo,Andamanese
1,Aka-Bo is an extinct language of India. It belongs to the Andamanese...,https://www.ethnologue.com/language/akm/?cf=1&token=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9..Req-XDtSF1tXvzfve3OtV68TJOqY8ZJDNyg5YfWBgsE,This graph shows the vitality of Aka-Bo. Institutional — The language has been developed to the point that it is used and sustained by institutions beyond the home and community.,Aka-Bo,Andamanese
2,The Great Andamanese and the Extinction of Bo | MANAS,https://southasia.ucla.edu/social-life/social-fabric-life/great-andamanese-extinction-bo/,"An indescribable feeling of sadness crept over me when I read some days ago of the passing away of Boa Sr., the last known speaker of Bo (also known as Aka-Bo and Ba), one of ten languages belonging to the Great Andamanese group.",Aka-Bo,Andamanese
3,Andamanese.org - Language Structure,https://www.andamanese.org/great-andamanese/language/language-structure,"Hence, Aka-Cari (Map 2), a North Great Andamanese language, was mutually unintelligible with Aka Bea, the southern variety. The present-day Great Andamanese language is a mixture of four northern varieties 3 with sporadic interferences from the central variety such as Aka Pucikwar.",Aka-Bo,Andamanese
4,"Full text of ""A History Of Our Relations With The Andamanese Vol.2""",https://archive.org/stream/in.ernet.dli.2015.24181/2015.24181.A-History-Of-Our-Relations-With-The-Andamanese-Vol2_djvu.txt,"The friendly Andamanese with him said they were Jurrahwallas, who are not friendly to them, owing to an encounter some years ago between that Tribe on Rutland Island, and the Aka- _Bm-da, in which the Tarawa Chief w-as wounded.",Aka-Bo,Andamanese
5,Stream 07 Adhémar - It Has To Be Dark To See The Stars by Lett...,https://soundcloud.com/lett-records/07-adhemar-it-has-to-be-dark,"Cover photography: Philippe Fontana. ""kumugyĭ"" means ""5"" (for 5 years of Lett Records) in Aka-bo language. Aka-bo was an Andamanese language who has now disappear. Lot of love to all the artists who appears on this celebration.",Aka-Bo,Andamanese
6,The Lost Languages and Enduring Traditions of the Andaman Islands,https://www.matsyahavelock.com/island-insights/the-lost-languages-and-enduring-traditions-of-the-andaman-islands.html,"• Aka-Bo: The Great Andamanese language, Aka-Bo, became extinct in 2010, with the passing of its last known speaker. The loss of Aka-Bo was not just the end of a language but also of a cultural identity that had been carried forward through oral traditions. •",Aka-Bo,Andamanese
7,Boa Sr – Endangered Languages and Cultures,https://www.paradisec.org.au/blog/2010/02/boa-sr/,"Boa Sr was apparently the last speaker of the Great Andamanese language Bo (or Aka-Bo, described as extinct in the Ethnologue). According to Survival International there were around 5000 Great Andamanese in 1858 , when the British invaded the Andaman Islands.",Aka-Bo,Andamanese
8,How artist Manish Pushkale’s latest work in... - The Indian Express,https://indianexpress.com/article/express-sunday-eye/artist-manish-pushkale-latest-work-france-lost-language-through-visual-cues-9477864/,"Introduced to Aka-Bo during a lecture by literary scholar-activist Ganesh N Devy in 2019, Pushkale lamented the loss and deliberated how the extinct language could be translated into visuals.",Aka-Bo,Andamanese
9,"(PDF) Comrie, B. & R. Zamponi 2019 ‘Subgrouping and lexical...",https://www.academia.edu/40037639/Comrie_B_and_R_Zamponi_2019_Subgrouping_and_lexical_distance_in_the_Great_Andamanese_family_In_Wortschätze_und_Sprachwelten_Beiträge_zu_Sprachtypologie_kontrastiver_Wort_bzw_Wortschatzforschung_und_Pragmatik_ed_by_M_L_Kotin_Berlin_etc_Lang_pp_35_57,"-. 1888. Andamanese music, with notes on Oriental music and musical instru- ments.The present form of Great Andamanese (PGA for short) is a ‘koiné’ or ‘mixed’ language and derives its lexical resources from the four northern languages, Khora, Sare, Bo and Jeru.",Aka-Bo,Andamanese
